From: Tiger Kaovilai <passawit.kaovilai@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] feat(darwin): server mode on macOS

Server mode on Darwin, so a Mac can be the home server:

- src/platform/darwin/server_darwin.c: darwin_platform_run_server, a
  trimmed twin of linux_platform_run_server. Same libmqvpn calls, the
  bundled POSIX transport over one UDP socket, the control API
  (control_socket.c, already built on Darwin), and the same log lines
  ("TUN <name> configured: <ip> (mtu=N)", "mqvpn server ready") that
  supervisors parse.
- The tunnel is a utun: the kernel picks the unit (logged). utun is
  point-to-point, so the client subnets (IPv4 and, when set, IPv6) are
  routed to it with route(8) -interface; the routes go with the utun.
- No TUN offloads, UDP GSO/GRO or hybrid TCP egress lane (Linux-only);
  the egress fd callbacks stay unset, which the core treats as no lane.
- Forwarding, NAT and the firewall stay with the caller, as on Linux
  (tailbond's server-up hook: net.inet.ip.forwarding and a pf anchor).
- main.c dispatches --mode server to it on __APPLE__ instead of failing.

Signed-off-by: Tiger Kaovilai <passawit.kaovilai@gmail.com>
---
